From be34f7e5bdb32960a1c57a678ed8b5619f938ea2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anuj Gupta <anuj20.g@samsung.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 14:14:36 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 09/18] dma-buf: pin I/O contexts while releasing their maps
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

dma_buf_io_map_release_work(), added by patch 1 of "Add dmabuf read/write
via io_uring", protects ctx from being torn down while it still needs
ctx->dev_ops and ctx->dmabuf->resv to call unmap():

	if (WARN_ON_ONCE(refcount_read(&ctx->refs) == 0))
		return;
	refcount_inc(&ctx->refs);

Christian König flagged this exact check-then-increment as "usually
illegal" on v4: refcount_read() and refcount_inc() are two separate
operations, so nothing stops ctx->refs from reaching zero and being torn
down by another thread in the gap between them.

Move the pin to where it can be taken unconditionally: at map creation,
in dma_buf_io_init_map(), while ctx is known live because the caller
holding it is what is calling this function. Drop it, as before, once
the release worker's unmap() call returns. The seg_shift driver-bug
unwind path in dma_buf_io_create_map() takes the same pin via
init_map() and must now drop it too when abandoning that map.

This is a narrow, self-contained fix: it does not yet touch the shape of
map->refs itself, or when this pin is released relative to a lingering
importer's own reference to the map -- that is a larger restructuring
handled separately.

Signed-off-by: Anuj Gupta <anuj20.g@samsung.com>
---
 drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c b/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c
index d0494212fa84..12e37c5924af 100644
--- a/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c
+++ b/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c
@@ -57,12 +57,10 @@ static void dma_buf_io_map_release_work(struct work_struct *work)
 	struct dma_buf_io_ctx *ctx = map->ctx;
 	struct dma_buf *dmabuf = ctx->dmabuf;
 
-	/* the release path must wait for fences */
-	if (WARN_ON_ONCE(refcount_read(&ctx->refs) == 0))
-		return;
-
-	/* Prevent from destoying the ctx while unmapping */
-	refcount_inc(&ctx->refs);
+	/*
+	 * ctx is guaranteed alive here: dma_buf_io_init_map() pinned it for
+	 * this map's entire lifetime, and that pin is only dropped below.
+	 */
 
 	/*
 	 * There are no more requests using the map, we can signal the fence.
@@ -110,6 +108,20 @@ int dma_buf_io_init_map(struct dma_buf_io_ctx *ctx, struct dma_buf_io_map *map)
 		       atomic_inc_return(&ctx->fence_seq));
 	map->fence = fence;
 	map->ctx = ctx;
+
+	/*
+	 * Pin ctx alive for this map's entire lifetime: the release worker
+	 * above needs ctx->dev_ops and ctx->dmabuf->resv to call unmap(), and
+	 * it runs independently of whether any importer still holds a
+	 * reference to the map. Dropped by that worker once unmap() has
+	 * completed.
+	 *
+	 * Must be an unconditional reference taken up front, not the
+	 * WARN_ON_ONCE(refcount_read()==0) then refcount_inc() pattern this
+	 * function used to have here -- Christian König flagged exactly that
+	 * check-then-increment as "usually illegal" on v4.
+	 */
+	refcount_inc(&ctx->refs);
 	return 0;
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_NS_GPL(dma_buf_io_init_map, "DMA_BUF");
@@ -153,6 +165,8 @@ struct dma_buf_io_map *dma_buf_io_create_map(struct dma_buf_io_ctx *ctx)
 		dma_fence_put(map->fence);
 		percpu_ref_exit(&map->refs);
 		kfree(map);
+		/* Drop this map's pin on ctx, taken in dma_buf_io_init_map(). */
+		dma_buf_io_ctx_put(ctx);
 		ret = -EFAULT;
 		goto out;
 	}
-- 
